WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.623
Do people have windows like a songbird's?

2
00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:08.043
And what does an age curve tell us?

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:14.853
In 1967, Eric Lenneberg proposed a critical period for language, from early

4
00:00:14.853 --> 00:00:15.853
childhood to puberty.

5
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:21.933
The main natural test is deaf children of hearing parents who first meet a signed

6
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:22.933
language late.

7
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:26.933
Delayed access to sign does affect later proficiency.

8
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:31.133
Mayberry compared adults who learned sign at the same age.

9
00:00:31.993 --> 00:00:34.293
Some already had English before losing their hearing.

10
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.697
Those with no accessible language before sign did worse.

11
00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:43.537
The groups also differed in when they lost hearing, so it is not a perfect control.

12
00:00:44.397 --> 00:00:48.657
But it suggests missing early language, not only age, leaves a lasting mark.

13
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.590
Johnson and Newport tested English grammar in adults who arrived in the United

14
00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.870
States at ages 3 to 39.

15
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:04.393
Predict. Plot adult grammar scores against age of arrival.

16
00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:07.513
Do scores hold until puberty, then drop?

17
00:01:08.353 --> 00:01:09.353
Or fall earlier?

18
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.990
Early arrivals scored like native speakers.

19
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.450
Then scores fell steadily with later arrival, up to about puberty.

20
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:26.573
Later arrivals scored lower, and varied widely.

21
00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:30.173
The outcome curve was graded, falling from childhood.

22
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:39.477
But a huge online grammar quiz suggested that grammar-learning ability stays high

23
00:01:39.477 --> 00:01:40.817
until about seventeen.

24
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:46.870
So how can a twelve-year-old arrive with full learning ability, yet end below native

25
00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:47.870
level?

26
00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:55.333
Grammar takes years to learn.

27
00:01:56.173 --> 00:01:59.573
A later starter gets fewer full-speed years before the slowdown.

28
00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:04.843
Suppose, as a toy, native-like grammar takes 7 full-speed years.

29
00:02:05.623 --> 00:02:07.223
A 12-year-old gets only 5.

30
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:11.053
If later learning is slow enough, that shortfall lasts.

31
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:15.487
So what lasts can start to fall before the ability to learn does.

32
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.640
A curve of what lasts is not a curve of learning ability.

33
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.200
Why learning slows is still debated.

34
00:02:23.283 --> 00:02:28.083
In sight, infants treated for dense cataracts showed some improvement in acuity

35
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:29.683
after as little as an hour.

36
00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:35.467
Yet processing of the spacing between facial features stayed impaired years later.

37
00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:38.207
Separate abilities have separate windows.

38
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.470
So ask what input arrives, at what age, and what lasts.
